{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAQpgF31Aop4A1RZZhMq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I changed `Hijack()` in `response_writer.go`. It used to refuse whenever the response was marked as written, and that included the case where only the headers had been sent. Now it refuses only after body bytes have been written (`w.size > 0`). That means a handler can send just the headers (for example a 101 Switching Protocols, as websocket libraries do) and still hijack the connection. Writing body data first still returns `errHijackAlreadyWritten`.\n\nI added a test case \"hijack after header write should succeed\" to `TestResponseWriterHijackAfterWrite`. The gate check passed: the test fails without the fix and passes with it.\n\nI didn't run the full test suite or try it with a real websocket library."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1337,"cache_read_input_tokens":33288,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1337,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":229,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c243c713-4efd-49bb-9bd0-8fdde9477ec7","uuid":"ee96f252-b963-4ddd-901d-ae77500df8ce","timestamp":"2026-10-18T21:27:42.243Z","request_id":"req_011CgAQpg2se6UjsSZsxbwi8"}
